00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.410
So go to patreon.com/wisecrack

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.820
or click the link in the
description to join today.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.930
Oh, and did we mention ad-free videos?

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:38.600
That's patreon.com/wisecrack.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.570
And now back to the show.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.117
And now a quick recap.

00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:44.690
"Wonder Woman 1984" finds Diana living

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.440
in that glitzy decade
working as an archeologist

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.720
at the Smithsonian.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.730
There, she and her anxious,
supposedly unlikeable

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.630
new friend Barbara study a
seemingly worthless stone

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.510
that promises to grant wishes.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.070
Each lady makes a wish.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.640
Diana that her dead boo
Steve was still alive,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.040
and Barbara that she was more like Diana.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.690
And surprise, their wishes come true,

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.390
but the stone is stolen
by badman Max Gold,

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:10.630
who basically wishes
for a thousand wishes.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:15.630
- I wish to be you,
the Dream Stone itself.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.900
- But, of course, there's a catch.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.200
The stone giveth and it also taketh.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.000
Diana loses her powers,
Barbara her humanity,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.540
and Max the moist air
entering his nostrils.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.380
Oh, and maybe also his kid.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.870
Then Max goes full Oprah on wish granting

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.740
sending the world into chaos,

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.720
and it's up to Wonder Woman to stop him,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.699
not with violence but with

00:02:35.699 --> 00:02:37.750
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:38.940
the truth.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.230
Now, lots of people have
their various reasons

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.150
for liking or disliking this film.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.010
But we think one of the best ways to look

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:47.994
at the film's flaws is through
its central narrative engine.

00:02:47.994 --> 00:02:49.210
- The monkey's paw.

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.330
- Now, it doesn't take
a Film Studies degree

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.430
to see what the film is trying to say.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.330
It's pretty clearly telegraphed
in the opening flashback,

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.690
which sees a young Diana
try to win an Amazonian race

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.160
by taking a shortcut.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.480
Sneaky shortcuts, Robin Wright warns us,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.660
equal very, very bad.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:06.583
- You cheated, Diana.

00:03:06.583 --> 00:03:08.016
- But, but that-

00:03:08.016 --> 00:03:10.550
- That is the truth.

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:13.470
That is the only truth
and truth is all there is.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.410
- [Helen] Anyway, this
quickly becomes a lens

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:18.590
to view the greed-is-good
mentality of the 1980s

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:19.960
or America's general love

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.570
of get rich quick schemes and grifters.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.070
- [Max] Do you have
everything you've ever wanted?

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.680
Aren't you tired of always
wishing you had more?

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:30.513
- [Helen] Get it?

00:03:30.513 --> 00:03:32.380
Cheating your way into
wealth is just as bad

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.070
as cheating in an Amazon race,

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.890
and it's up to Diana to
set the record straight.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:40.963
- This world was a beautiful
place just as it was.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:44.483
And you cannot have it all.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.450
You can only have the truth.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.100
- Now for many at Wisecrack,
this moment felt a little dumb.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.730
Like after hearing those words,

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.580
Bernie Madoff wept a single tear

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.620
and apologized to all of his victims.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:00.110
But part of the reason
it might have felt hollow

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.530
is the nature of the wishing stone itself.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:05.240
Supposedly created by some sinister god,

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.090
all we know is that it is indeed a trick

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.450
which gives you your wish
while also taking away

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.270
something very important to you.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.720
In this way, the stone
is indeed a monkey's paw,

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.310
as Steve rightly points out
while casually possessing

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.140
the apartment, body, and genitals

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.450
of an innocent, unsuspecting engineer.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:24.680
But what even is a monkey's paw?

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.640
Well, that term just so happens to come

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.530
from a short story of the same name

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.850
by British writer W.W. Jacobs.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:32.310
Published in 1902,

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.410
the tale chronicles the unfortunate events

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.780
that befall one family
after they wish upon

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.040
their friend's shriveled
little monkey appendage.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.900
Despite being warned by said friend

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.540
to pitch it onto the fire
again like a sensible man,

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.000
the patriarch of the family,

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.970
who notes that he has basically
everything he needs in life,

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.270
wishes for 200 pounds,
because why dream big, right?

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:54.310
The next day their son
dies in a factory accident

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:56.170
and the couple receives 200 pounds

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:58.340
in whoopsies money from his employers.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:00.560
The distraught matriarch
convinces her husband

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.710
to use the second wish to
bring their son back to life.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:06.880
But upon hearing the zombified
son knocking at the door,

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.190
the spooked-out dad uses the
third wish to kill him again.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:13.400
Yes, that's the end, and
the moral of the story.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.000
Careful what you wish for.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.770
The whole thing is basically a screed

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.470
about not messing with fate.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:20.820
If the Old Man upstairs
wants you to have 200 bucks

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.170
to blow one Funko Pops,

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.310
he would have already turned
your hot water back on.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:27.290
It has since, obviously, become a trope.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.130
- This aftershave made women want me,

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.040
but it also made me impotent.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.910
- So how does "Wonder
Woman" botch this trope?

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:36.970
Well, if we consider the
original simian wish fulfiller,

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.550
some of "Wonder Woman's"
problems reveal themselves.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.010
In the story, the father's wish is granted

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.500
at the literal expense of his son's death,

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.600
thus punishing him for
wishing for anything

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:47.670
in the first place.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:50.150
His son dies, he earns 200 bucks.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:51.560
Wish granted, you dummy.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.760
And next time, don't (beep) with fate.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.500
The monkey's paw often works best

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.150
when it uses narrative
events to show the character

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.700
that the thing they want, i.e. wish for,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.048
is not the thing they need, i.e. lose.

00:06:04.048 --> 00:06:07.715
- I lost (crying) my greed.

00:06:09.397 --> 00:06:10.564
I deserved it.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.510
- Now, that's fitting because
a key screenwriting rule

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:17.800
is to establish your character's
want versus their need.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:19.710
Your poor character spends the whole movie

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.270
pursuing their want
only to learn at the end

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:24.730
that the conflicting
need is more important.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:27.900
Like how in the 1988
Tom Hanks classic "Big"

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.080
he wants to grow up.

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:30.520
- I wish I were big.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:32.820
- [Helen] But needs to learn
to appreciate his childhood.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.960
Monkey's paws are a supernatural
vehicle to get characters

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:39.660
to realize, sometimes
tragically, what they really need

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:41.920
instead of what they originally want.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:44.120
And this brings us to the
thing about "Wonder Woman"

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.670
that kind of bums us out.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.620
That's because the movie has very little

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:48.720
of the cause and effect

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.470
that can make monkey's
paws stories compelling.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.360
The Bilalian King of Crude's dilemma

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:55.130
is probably the closest we get

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.340
to a real monkey's paw situation.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:59.170
He asks to get his land back.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:00.960
This results in a wall being built.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.240
- I wish for things one cannot attain.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:04.287
- Like what?

00:07:04.287 --> 00:07:07.350
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.250
- All of my land to be returned.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:12.450
- Wish granted but that
wall cuts tons of people off

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:14.060
from their homes and water supply

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:15.780
and makes them want to kill him.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:18.720
We get real narrative
consequences of his actions,

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.250
a real careful what you wish for

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.320
with some extra poetic justice.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.210
But even that's a little sloppy.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.620
The movie slaps on the
additional consequence

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:28.600
of Max stealing his security team.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.210
Diana's trade-off is a
little more nonsensical.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.517
It seems like the
filmmakers wanted to say,

00:07:33.517 --> 00:07:35.087
"Diana wants her lover back,

00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:36.607
"but she needs to accept the truth

00:07:36.607 --> 00:07:38.517
"and not cheat at anything ever.

00:07:38.517 --> 00:07:40.057
"And I swear to god, Diana,

00:07:40.057 --> 00:07:42.117
"if you even think about
hustling men at bars

00:07:42.117 --> 00:07:43.607
"with arm wrestling challenges,

00:07:43.607 --> 00:07:46.730
"the universe will take
away your power so fast."

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:48.370
If bringing back your dead lover

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:51.700
and consequently losing your
powers seems a little random,

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:52.890
well, it is.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.880
The idea is basically just
lazily shoe horning the story

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:57.710
into the classic superhero dilemma

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.770
of how do you get laid more
than once every 50 years

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:02.920
and also successfully saving the world?

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:05.580
Now, this dilemma is perfectly
embodied in the narrative

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:07.770
of Tobey Maguire-era "Spider-Man,"

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.560
which puts superheroing
into inherent conflict

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.210
with being with Mary Jane.

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:16.360
- [Peter] She looks at me
every day, Mary Jane Watson.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:17.560
Oh boy.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.560
If she only knew how I felt about her,

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:21.690
but she can never know.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:24.410
- [Helen] But in this film,
it doesn't actually develop.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:26.610
Diana's wish doesn't
cause a chain of events

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:28.380
that happen to take away her powers.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.520
It's just a magical trade-off
we have to kind of accept.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.740
And so, when Diana ugly
cries over giving up Steve

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:37.614
and instantly becoming
strong, it feels kind of meh.

00:08:37.614 --> 00:08:39.300
(sweeping instrumental music)
- I can't say goodbye.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:40.800
- For the film's main characters,

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.620
the monkey's paw functions less as

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.430
a be careful what you wish
for because it might come true

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:48.560
and much more of a haphazard
trading one good thing

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.060
for one random (beep) thing.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:52.220
This is pretty clear in
Barbara's trajectory.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:55.570
Now early on, her character is
depicted as a pathetic loser

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:57.490
who magically becomes hot, likable,

00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:59.060
and able to walk in heels.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:00.950
But for wishing to be like Diana,

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:02.640
the monkey's paw takes its payment

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.350
in the form of stealing her humanity,

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:09.170
aka turning her into a
stone cold, violent asshole.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:11.320
But what's her sin in the first place?

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:14.020
Accidentally wishing to
be an immortal superhero?

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.680
See, her initial wish,

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.510
unlike asking for a nuclear
arsenal or free money,

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:20.712
seems so much less problematic.

00:09:20.712 --> 00:09:22.412
- I do you know what I'd wish for.

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:27.050
To be like Diana.

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:29.210
- She doesn't want to be suddenly famous.

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:30.940
She just wants to be strong, sexy,

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:32.470
and liked by her coworkers,

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.290
not even in a "Black Mirror"-esque,

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:36.120
obsessed with your social score way.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.240
Just like a normal human way.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.930
Her powers are completely
accidental to the wish.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:43.000
So what really is the message?

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:45.880
That she needs to just accept
being sad, lonely Barbara

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.690
about whom the office
bros needlessly talk shit?

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.960
Now maybe the message is
that Barbara needs to learn

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.470
self-confidence the hard way,

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:54.270
by scrolling through
the social skills Reddit

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.050
or going to an out-of-network
therapist once a week.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.500
But the film doesn't say that.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.520
Instead, Diana is all-

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:02.280
- 'Cause you're not the only one

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:07.050
who imagined a world were
everything is different.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:08.680
- [Helen] Basically,
the message feels like

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.980
Barbara should have just
been content with her lot.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.700
Again, just kind of weird
and even a little mean.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.570
And what, only Amazonians
get to be popular?

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:18.800
It's worth noting that
the monkey's paw mechanics

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.200
at the end of the film
are a little bit better.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.440
People want unearned stuff with no regards

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:25.640
for the consequences and, en masse,

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.700
this narratively launches
the world into a nuclear war.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:30.620
- Soviets' early detection
system have picked up

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:31.910
our new nukes.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:32.900
- [Woman] What new nukes?

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:36.290
- 100 warheads just went
online ready to launch.

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:38.410
They're taking this as an act of war,

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.090
prepping to return fire.

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:42.660
- So greed is bad. Yay.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.860
But perhaps the strangest thing of all,

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:46.880
there actually is a pretty interesting

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.940
monkey's paw situation for Diana

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:50.530
that's baked right into the plot.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:52.250
Now, we're not typically in the business

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:55.480
of suggesting punch ups,
but indulge us this once.

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.940
The screenwriter's choice
to bring Steve back to life

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:00.730
in another man's body has
confounded the internet

00:11:00.730 --> 00:11:03.210
and made us a little
existentially concerned.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:06.170
Is this guy still alive while
this stone cold hottie lives,

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:08.120
breathes, and sexes in his body?

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.430
Is he stuck in the white
man's sunken place?

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:12.940
It's never explained and Wonder Woman,

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:14.860
despite supposedly being the greatest,

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:16.610
most moralist person in the world,

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:18.170
seriously doesn't seem to give a (beep)

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.550
about romantically
borrowing the body of a dude

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:22.480
who is, for all intents and purposes,

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:24.210
spiritually blackout drunk.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:26.940
But it actually really easily
could have been explained.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:29.540
The real monkey's paw
consequence all along

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.210
could have been that Steve
possessing another man's body

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:34.070
was the careful what you wish for.

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:37.350
That is to say Diana's real
struggle could have been

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.930
whether or not she could
possibly live with herself

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.390
if she brought Steve back
to life at the expense of a,

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:45.410
by all accounts, perfectly
nice seeming engineer

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:46.820
with a messy apartment.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:48.220
She wants her boyfriend back,

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.270
but she needs to abide by her moral code

00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:52.390
of not doing a "Get Out."

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:55.360
- I want your eye, man.

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.430
- But the most interesting consequence

00:11:57.430 --> 00:11:59.630
of bringing Steve back to life is entirely

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:01.490
and very awkwardly ignored.

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:03.640
Instead, the stakes are incongruently

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:05.780
just about Diana losing her powers

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.530
and convincing everyone in
the world to accept their lot

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.650
because messing with
fate can ruin your body,

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:12.050
mind, soul, et cetera.

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:14.000
And for everyone else's wants and needs,

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.320
Barbara wants to be cool but
learns that she needs to accept

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:20.350
being a nerd because the
alternative is being a huge (beep).

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:23.440
Steve wants to be with Diana,
but he needs to be dead.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:25.170
Max Gold wants a ton of wealth

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:27.740
but learns that he needs his health?

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:29.740
Oh, and also on top of that,

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:31.260
he seems to randomly realize

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:33.080
that he also super needs his son,

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:35.220
a revelation that is basically mandated

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:36.970
by Diana's lasso of truth.

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:40.120
So not exactly the most
exciting character growth.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:41.990
While the film has been
hailed in some reviews

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:44.990
as being hopeful and
optimistic about human nature,

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:47.470
all we're really seeing
is a superhero film

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:50.130
in which the superhero makes
the world a better place

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:53.180
by magically convincing all
humans to stop being greedy

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:54.920
over the span of a mediocre speech

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:57.150
that sounds like a yoga
teacher's Instagram caption.

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:58.910
I mean, if your suspension of disbelief

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:01.590
wasn't already broken, it surely was now.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:03.950
We're not saying that every superhero film

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:06.530
needs to be cranky about
human nature in the end,

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:08.770
but this movie puts a rose-colored lens

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:10.440
over an ultimately kind of,

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:13.040
no, actually very cynical worldview,

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:15.930
which is that we should accept
the (beep) of our given lives

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:17.437
because as Diana puts it.

00:13:17.437 --> 00:13:19.023
- And you cannot have it all.

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:22.510
You can only have the truth.

00:13:24.407 --> 00:13:26.000
And the truth is enough.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:27.940
- [Helen] The film wants
to put forward a critique

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:30.550
of American avarice,
get rich quick schemes,

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:32.130
and the hucksters that run them,

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:33.860
but it needs to figure out how to do that

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:37.200
so that wishing to be
likable isn't a cardinal sin.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.270
What's more, Diana's speech
just feels kind of off.

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:43.260
The truth in this imaginary 1984 world

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.040
certainly doesn't seem beautiful.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.500
The president is a doddering
fool who just wants more nukes.

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:51.240
- What is there to wish for but more?

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:53.350
More nuclear weapons, more than they have.

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:55.090
Closer to them.

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:57.057
If I just had that in place

00:13:57.057 --> 00:13:58.680
then they would to have to listen to us.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:00.847
- [Helen] The Middle East is
a very simplistically rendered

00:14:00.847 --> 00:14:04.010
(beep) show and there's
plenty of domestic violence

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:04.843
for good measure.

00:14:04.843 --> 00:14:06.180
The world may be pretty beautiful

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.330
if you're a hot superhero Amazonian,

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:11.910
but expecting everyone
else to agree feels like-

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:15.330
- Even now, you're patronizing me.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:18.470
- Did "Wonder Woman 1984"
miss the proverbial mark?

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:20.760
And if so, was it because of the bizarre

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:22.540
monkey's paw plot line?

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:25.770
Or are we just grumpy because
of the (beep) vaccine rollout?

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:27.080
Let us know in the comments.

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:29.250
Big thanks to all our
patrons for your support.

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:31.530
Hit that subscribe button
like you're Kristen Wiig

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:34.480
pretty viciously assaulting
a low-key sex predator

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.610
and ring that bell like
it's still Christmas.

00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:39.256
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:14:39.256 --> 00:14:40.359
Peace.

00:14:40.359 --> 00:14:44.109
